Third-party dependencies carry their own licenses. Read them from the resolved
graph rather than a list here, which would go stale on the next bump:

    cargo metadata --format-version 1 | jq -r '.packages[] | "\(.name) \(.version) \(.license)"'

Two of them are worth naming because they reach further than a normal
dependency:

  - The kernel crate vendors the six x86 port-I/O instructions it issues
    (`kernel/src/arch/x86_port.rs`) rather than depending on an assembly crate,
    so no third party is involved in the hardware surface.
  - `lz4` and `zstd` wrap the reference C implementations. They are optional,
    behind the `std` feature, and nothing outside that feature links them.
